# Reflection 3 — LLM-Assisted SQL & Query Profiling

> Module 3 · Session 3 · [Module 3 portfolio page](../module-03/README.md)

## Take-away — what I want to remember, and my advice

**`SEARCH` in a query plan means the engine used an index, not that using it was a good idea.** The slowest plan I measured all module was made *entirely* of `SEARCH` lines — no `SCAN` anywhere — and it was four times slower than the plan it replaced. I went in reading `SCAN` as "bad" and `SEARCH` as "fixed", and that is simply not what the words mean. `SCAN` means every row was read; whether that is bad depends on how many rows you needed.

The second thing, which I expect to be more useful long-term: **an index is not a property of a table.** It is a relationship between a table and a particular predicate. `ix_orderline_product` did nothing for the dashboard query and gave a 10× speedup on a query filtering `product_id` directly — same index, same table, same rows. "Useless index" was the wrong conclusion and "useless *for this query*" was the right one, and the difference between those two sentences is most of what I learned.

**My advice to someone starting this module:** run `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` before you measure anything, and write the plan down even when it is boring. The plans that did not change are what let me tell a real improvement from noise — the dashboard got 18% faster with an index that provably did nothing, and without the before-plan I would have written that up as a win.

## What was good

Predicting before running changed how I read the output. When the prediction and the plan agreed I learned nothing, and when they disagreed I had to go find out why — which is how I ended up on SQLite's `LIKE` optimization conditions rather than just noting "it scanned" and moving on.

Putting the measurements in a script (`bench.py`, then `evidence.py`) felt like overkill at the time and was the best decision I made. Re-running the whole benchmark after each index became one command, the numbers stayed comparable, and the script itself ended up being portfolio evidence. Doing it by hand in the shell would have produced numbers I could not defend.

## What was difficult, where I struggled

**The honest answer is that the hardest part was not SQL.** I came back to this module after a long gap and lost most of a session to environment problems before writing a single query:

- I typed shell commands at the `sqlite>` prompt and could not work out why nothing happened. Two programs, two prompts, and I did not notice the prompt had changed.
- I had **two clones** of my own portfolio repo on the machine, in `Documents/` and `Documents/GitHub/`, both behind the remote, and I did not know which one I had been pushing from.
- I had also, at some earlier point, run commands from my home folder instead of inside the repo, so there were orphaned `module-01/`, `src/` and `uv-check.py` copies sitting in `~` that belonged to nothing.

The lesson is unglamorous and I am writing it down anyway: **check `pwd` before running anything.** Every instruction in these modules assumes you are at the repository root, and none of them tell you that, because it is obvious to whoever wrote them.

The genuinely difficult *technical* part was explaining **why** adding an index made a query slower, as opposed to observing that it did. The observation took one run of the benchmark. Working out that the second index made an orders-driven plan viable, which in turn activated the first index, which replaced one sequential 240,000-row read with ~243,000 scattered ones, took considerably longer and is the only part of this module I would not want to be asked about cold.

## Good or fun struggling / exploration

The `LIKE 'Bei%'` result was a proper rabbit hole and the best hour of the module. The rule everyone teaches is "leading wildcard can't use an index, trailing wildcard can" — and I had just watched `LIKE '%ei%'` scan for exactly that reason, so I was confident. Then `LIKE 'Bei%'` scanned too, with the index sitting right there.

The cause turned out to have nothing to do with wildcards: SQLite's `LIKE` is case-insensitive for ASCII by default, a plain index is built with `BINARY` collation, and an index sorted `Bei…` before `bei…` cannot answer a case-insensitive prefix query. Two fixes, both of which I tested: `PRAGMA case_sensitive_like = ON`, or build the index `COLLATE NOCASE`. Either brings back a `SEARCH` — and the plan reveals it as a *range* scan, `(technician>? AND technician<?)`, because a prefix match is a range between `'Bei'` and `'Bej'`.

What I like about this one is that the rule I was applying was almost right. It is conditional on things nobody mentions when teaching it, and I would have stated it as a flat fact in a checkpoint.

**Still not sure about:** the `CO-ROUTINE (subquery-2)` lines in the window-function plan. I can see the query works and I cannot explain what a co-routine is doing there. Going on the list as an honest "I don't know what this does".

